# Normalize line endings on commit, check out native on Windows
* text=auto

*.cs      text diff=csharp
*.csproj  text
*.sln     text eol=crlf
*.json    text
*.md      text

# A shell script is read by bash, which takes a carriage return for part of the command.
# Git for Windows' bash happens to forgive it; WSL reading the same checkout does not.
*.sh      text eol=lf

# Bytes that must arrive exactly as they left. `text=auto` above guesses, and it guesses
# right today - every PDF and font here is detected as binary - but a PDF is mostly
# ASCII with byte offsets in it, and a line-ending conversion anywhere in one moves every
# offset after it and breaks the cross-reference table. That is not a risk worth leaving
# to a heuristic, so it is said.
*.pdf     binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.ttc     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary

# What is generated says so.
#
# This repository tracks a great deal of it on purpose - the schema because it is a
# published contract, tests/output because it is the evidence the checks produced, the
# standalone pages because they are what somebody downloads. None of it is written by
# hand, and none of it is worth reading in a diff.
#
# Measured before writing this: 18.4 MB of HTML and SVG against 1.5 MB of C#, nearly
# all of it under tests/output. Left unmarked, GitHub's language bar would call a
# layout engine a HTML project by about nine to one - a first impression that is not
# merely unflattering but wrong, and the kind of claim this file is otherwise full of
# warnings about.
#
# linguist-generated does both jobs: out of the language statistics, and collapsed in
# a diff rather than printed.
tests/output/**                     linguist-generated=true
examples/schema/*.html              linguist-generated=true
examples/schema/*.schema.json       linguist-generated=true
release/**                          linguist-generated=true
